      "snippet": {
        "text": "The use of AI chatbots in higher education is more established, but their integration into general education is also becoming increasingly popular (Mai et al., 2024). Existing studies have explored chatbot applications across a variety of subjects, with a consensus that these tools offer significant opportunities for enhancing traditional teaching, such as providing foundational mathematics knowledge (Wardat et al., 2023), supporting the design of a 5E-based 7th grade science unit (Cooper, 2023), supporting the development of course materials in geography (Day, 2023), history (Jauhiainen & Guerra, 2023), and physics (K€ uchemann et al., 2023). These supports reduce teachers' professional workload and allow them to focus on activities that guide students' higher-order thinking (Limna et al., 2023). Overall, existing research agrees that integrating AI chatbots can promote a more effective, efficient, and interactive general education system. \n\nAlthough chatbot tools show significant advantages in supporting the professional work of general education teachers, there are still concerns related to the use of these tools that have been pointed out in existing studies, such as many inaccurate information in biology (Clark, 2023), falsified citations in geography (Day, 2023), lack of understanding in complex mathematical knowledge (Wardat et al., 2023), superficiality of designed science unit materials (Cooper, 2023), and many other challenges. These challenges highlight the indispensable role of teachers in ensuring the authenticity of content and their important participation in classroom practices. This means that while chatbots can support teaching practices, they cannot replace the role of human educators in the classroom. In the AI-powered educational ecosystem, chatbots take on four roles as interlocutors, content providers, teaching assistants, and evaluators (Jeon & Lee, 2023). Meanwhile, teachers' roles are redefined into three key functions: coordinating diverse resources with high-quality pedagogical decisions, encouraging students to become active investigators, and enhancing students' ethical awareness about AI (Jeon & Lee, 2023). As AI chatbots become increasingly integrated into teachers' teaching practices, presenting both advantages and challenges, it is essential to consider factors related to their perceived usability and credibility.",
