      "snippet": {
        "text": "Scientific inquiry, a fundamental process for scientific advancement, is critical in shaping students' critical thinking and problem-solving skills while fostering an appreciation for scientific methods, which are becoming increasingly significant in today's world. Involvement in the field of science education as teachers, researchers, educators, and policymakers is essential to comprehending and advancing this method. The article 'Completing the Progression: Establishing an International Baseline of Primary, Middle, and Secondary Students' Views of Scientific Inquiry' (Lederman et al., 2024) provides essential insights into students' understanding of scientific inquiry across different educational levels and regions. Establishing a comprehensive baseline through this international study not only allows you to benchmark your students' understanding against global standards but also emphasizes your role in a global community of teachers, educators, researchers, and policymakers. It assists in identifying gaps and strengths to inform better school science curriculum design and ensure targeted and effective professional development programs for enhancing teacher quality and development in the future. The findings from Lederman et al. (2021) and Lederman et al. (2024) reveal that students' conceptions of the nature of scientific inquiry (NOSI) are largely dependent on their educational stage. Primary school students often exhibit a simplistic view, focusing primarily on basic observations and straightforward experiments. Middle and secondary school students, although possessing a slightly broader understanding, often remain constrained by confirmatory approaches to experimentation. The aforementioned constraints underscore the urgent necessity for progressive and stage-appropriate methods of instruction that foster a developing and sophisticated comprehension of scientific inquiry (Gai et al., 2022;Lederman et al., 2024). By establishing an incremental 