      "snippet": {
        "text": "AI is utilized in STEM education in diverse ways, engaging students and educators in teaching and learning. As a follow-up to prior work, Xu and Ouyang (2022) identified six categories of AI applications in STEM education: learning prediction, intelligent tutoring systems, student behavior detection, automation, educational robots, and miscellaneous others. AI technologies can enhance student engagement, improve learning outcomes, and provide valuable insights for educators (Nagaraj et al., 2023) in STEM education. Further, AI-driven learning analytics enable data-driven decision-making in curriculum design and assessment practices (Nuangchalerm, 2023). This can be seen in How and Hung's (2019) study of two AI-data-based approaches to predictively simulate how concrete mixture inputs might affect the output of compressive strength under different conditions among STEAM learners, giving students the ability to improve their AI literacy, ask better questions, and enhance their problemsolving skills with advanced data analytics. \n\nIn another example, generative AI (GenAI)'s ability to have open conversations with students about STEMrelated topics has made it an attractive tool to use as an \"object to think with\" (Rodrigues Vasconcelos & dos Santos, 2023, p. 3). Students may use chatbots, such as ChatGPT or Google Gemini, to enhance their understanding of a concept through an interactive inquiry approach, as if speaking to a disciplinary expert. This engagement has the potential to enhance metacognitive skills, critical thinking, and problemsolving abilities that foster a deeper comprehension of the material in STEM (Rodrigues Vasconcelos & dos Santos, 2023) while serving as an intelligent tutor. \n\nIn addition, research has pointed to the potential use of the previously mentioned applications, like ChatGPT, in K-12 classrooms by educators (Crompton & Burke, 2024;Rodrigues Vasconcelos & dos Santos, 2023;Rudolph et al., 2023). It has been suggested 