      "snippet": {
        "text": "AI technology represents a larger category of tools encompassing diverse technologies and methodologies, including machine learning, natural language processing, data mining, neural networks, and algorithms (Agarwall, 2018;Baker & Smith, 2019). Integrating AI technology into K-12 educational environments has become a recent subject of interest for educators seeking innovative approaches to enhance teaching and learning (Crompton et al., 2022). Crompton et al. (2022) conducted a systematic review to examine the affordances and challenges of AIEd in K-12 over ten years, from 2011 to 2021. They found that AIEd offers several educational affordances, including pedagogy, administrative tasks, and content knowledge development. Further, AI technologies such as intelligent tutoring systems, adaptive learning platforms, and generative AI (GenAI) tools encourage students to engage in active learning, problem-solving, and critical thinking (Huang et al., 2021;Xu & Ouyang, 2022). \n\nResearchers have acknowledged the complexities of aligning AI with particular learning theories (Diaz & Delgado, 2024). Despite these complexities, Ouyang and Jiao (2021) conceptualized AIEd applications as categories belonging to three paradigms: AI-directed, AI-supported, and AI-empowered. With the first paradigm, learners are characterized as recipients of AI services, like an intelligent tutoring system whereby AI serves as the guide for learning processes. The learner-as-recipient approach is framed in behaviorism, where the \"behavior becomes a reflex response to stimulus\" (Rubens et al., 2014, p.173). The second paradigm, AI-supported, reflects that learners engage as collaborators with AI and are guided by cognitive and social constructivism frameworks. This suggests that learners actively construct their knowledge through interactions with their environment and assimilating new experiences (Kahn & Winters, 2021). This theory is particularly relevant as it relates to the 