      "snippet": {
        "text": "Learning is a stateful subjective process of navigating struggles, including moments of impasse [27]. For students to progress and overcome impasses they cannot resolve alone, they require appropriate scaffolding from an external source [28]. Educational chatbots driven by Large Language Models (LLMs) can serve as this scaffolded intervention during learning [20,25]. While these chatbots constitute an opportunity, such as increased efficiency [7,18,20], they also carry risks such as undesirable cognitive offloading [25,30]). Research is required to understand the risks and benefits of chatbots. \n\nThe effectiveness of LLM chatbots during learning depends on its inputs, which are driven by the user prompts, and in the case of educational chatbots, student prompts [2]. Therefore, research into chatbot effectiveness requires an understanding of student prompts, which often take the form of questions. However, educational literature indicates students often struggle to verbalise their needs through prompts to LLM chatbots [22,31], mirroring difficulties in articulating questions observed in traditional human-driven and Intelligent Tutoring Systems (ITS) tutoring [3,13]. Therefore, to gain insights into how students prompt LLM chatbots, this paper analyses student questions from two distinct STEM (Science, Technology, Engineering, and Mathematics) learning contexts: formative problem-solving self-study in engineering, and summative coursework task in computing. \n\nSchemas can be used to classify student questions and have been developed and applied in the fields of human tutoring and Intelligent Tutoring Systems (ITS) [3,6,13,16]. For example, under such schemas question can be classified as 'procedural' (\"how do we derive it\"), 'causal' (\"why is pressure not a function of theta and z\"), 'definition' (\"what are the units for viscosity\"), or 'verification' (\"do we assume the pressure gradient is 0?\"). \n\nApplication of classification schemas onto student prompts to LLM-driven chatbots is an open area of research [2,19,23].",
