      "snippet": {
        "text": "The 5E Instructional Model emerged as a powerful tool for scaffolding scientific inquiry among multilingual learners in high school science classrooms. The 5E model consists of five phases: Engage, Explore, Explain, Elaborate, and Evaluate. In the context of multilingual learners, each phase provides specific opportunities for language development alongside content learning: \n\n• Engage: Activates prior knowledge and connects to students' cultural experiences. \n\n• Explore: Allows hands-on investigation, promoting collaborative dialogue. \n\n• Explain: Encourages students to articulate their understanding, supporting academic language development. \n\n• Elaborate: Extends concepts to new situations, reinforcing both content and language. \n\n• Evaluate: Assesses understanding through various modalities, accommodating diverse linguistic abilities. Dodge [30] implemented a 3-week intervention using the 5E model in 9th-grade environmental science classes. The mixed-methods study, involving 48 students including English language learners, revealed increased student engagement and improved transfer of knowledge. Particularly beneficial for diverse learners, including ELLs, the study demonstrated significant improvements in both content understanding and student participation. This suggests that the model supports both conceptual learning and active engagement in high school science settings, which can be especially valuable for multilingual learners developing academic language skills alongside scientific knowledge. \n\nThe findings in this study collectively demonstrate that effective strategies for supporting multilingual learners in high school science often address multiple aspects of learning simultaneously. They tend to support content understanding while also promoting language development, engagement in scientific practices, and feelings of belonging in science. The diversity of approaches highlights the importance of using a variety of strategies to meet the needs of different learners and contexts.",
