      "snippet": {
        "text": "The purpose of this literature review is to examine recent evidence-based instructional interventions in science education for emergent multilingual learners in high school settings, focusing on the period from 2012 to 2023. The review aims to identify and analyze instructional strategies and approaches that support the development of robust scientific understanding and disciplinary literacy among multilingual high school students. \n\nTo address the research questions, I conducted a comprehensive, multistep literature review. The process began with electronic searches using a combined search engine that encompassed several standard educational research databases, including ERIC, Academic Search Complete, Education Full Text, PsycINFO, Education Research Complete, and Psychology and Behavioral Science Collection. My search strategy employed a three-tiered approach. Primary search terms included various forms of \"instructional interventions.\" Secondary search terms comprised a comprehensive list of labels commonly applied to English Learners (ELs), such as \"English learner,\" \"English language learner,\" \"emergent bilingual,\" and \"multilingual learners.\" Tertiary search terms focused on \"high school science education\" and its various subdisciplines (e.g., biology, chemistry, physics). \n\nThe initial search yielded 412 articles, which were presented in order of relevance. I then conducted a preliminary screening of titles and abstracts to determine relevance to the research focus. This initial screening reduced the number of potential articles to 87. When the relevance of a study was unclear from the title and abstract alone, I skimmed the full manuscript to determine its inclusion status. \n\nStudies needed to meet several criteria to be included in the review. They had to be peer-reviewed and conducted between 2012 and 2023. The only exception is Stoddart et al.'s study [27] which was conducted in 2002, which provided valuable insights on combining inquiry-based science instruction with language development strategies. I included empirical studies, intervention reports, and systematic reviews that focused on instructional strategies for multilingual learners in high school science education. I excluded theoretical papers without empirical data, studies outside the specified date range, and those not specifically addressing science education for multilingual learners at the secondary level. \n\nFollowing the initial screening, I conducted a full-text review of the remaining 87 articles. This more thorough examination assessed the quality of research methods, clarity of intervention descriptions, and relevance of findings to the research questions.",
