      "snippet": {
        "text": "The literature review revealed several instructional strategies and interventions that are effective in supporting emergent multilingual learners in high school science education. \"Effective\" in this context refers to strategies that demonstrate positive outcomes in one or more of the following areas: science content understanding, language development, engagement in scientific practices, and fostering a sense of belonging in science. The specific outcomes varied across studies, as detailed below. \n\n1. Leveraging linguistic resources: translanguaging, interactive science notebooks, and think-pair-share \n\nThese strategies focus on utilizing students' full linguistic repertoires to enhance learning in high school science classrooms. Translanguaging techniques have shown particular promise in high school science settings. Licona and Kelly [16] explored translanguaging in a bilingual middle school science class, with findings that have direct implications for high school instruction. Their qualitative ethnographic study revealed that allowing students to flexibly use their full linguistic repertoire supported scientific argumentation and engagement with socio-scientific issues. In a high school context, González-Howard and McNeill [25] found that translanguaging practices in biology classrooms supported emergent bilingual students in engaging more deeply with complex scientific concepts and in developing robust arguments. \n\nBamiro [15] investigated the effects of guided discovery and think-pair-share strategies on secondary school students' achievement in chemistry in Nigeria. The study finds that both strategies significantly improve students' performance in chemistry, with guided discovery showing slightly higher effectiveness, while also noting that gender does not significantly influence achievement when using these strategies. Wilmes and Siry [24] found that students use interactive science notebooks as interactional spaces for plurilingual students to engage in science practices and language development. While not specifically targeting high school environments, this research offers a significant understanding of how students with diverse language backgrounds can participate in scientific activities and enhance their linguistic abilities.",
