      "snippet": {
        "text": "This is further supported by metacognitive regulation theory (Flavell, 1979), which highlights the importance of selfmonitoring and reflective thinking in evaluating complex information. AI-generated text often exhibits fluency illusions-seemingly coherent but incorrect responses that can mislead less proficient readers. Thus, students with stronger writing and interpretive skills are better equipped to scrutinise AI-generated content, assess its credibility, and communicate inaccuracies effectively. This theoretical grounding supports the following Hypothesis 1b: \n\nHypothesis 1b: Students with better language skills are more likely to detect/articulate AI hallucination. \n\nBeyond academic and linguistic skills, cognitive biases and epistemic cognition shape students' interaction with AI-generated content. Research on risk perception and technology trust suggests that women exhibit greater caution toward AI (Aldasoro et al., 2024), aligning with metacognitive regulation theory (Flavell, 1979), which posits that individuals interpret ambiguous information based on prior beliefs and contextual cues. Epistemic cognition research further suggests that women are more likely to engage in reflective assessment of new information, questioning its validity rather than accepting it at face value (see a review on theories using social-psychological, anthropological-evolutionary, and medical science evidence by Meyers-Levy & Loken, 2015). This gender difference, termed \"epistemic vigilance\" (Sperber et al., 2010), enhances error detection, making female students more likely to scrutinise AI-generated responses for inconsistencies. In contrast, higher technological selfefficacy among men may lead to faster but less critical acceptance of AI outputs. These genderlinked variations in epistemic cognition provide a basis for Hypothesis 1c: \n\nHypothesis 1c: Female students are more likely to detect/articulate AI Hallucination. \n\nAI scepticism is another important cognitive factor influencing hallucination detection. \n\nEpistemic vigilance suggests that individuals who are naturally more sceptical engage in higher levels of cognitive scrutiny when encountering new information (Sperber et al., 2010). Students who are predisposed to questioning AI reliability are more likely to verify outputs, identify inconsistencies, and resist automation bias.",
