      "snippet": {
        "text": "Using data from a Year 2 econometrics course for economics and management students, we find a significant correlation between academic ability, critical thinking, and the capacity to detect and articulate concerns about AI-generated hallucinations. Despite having access to ample resources, only 20% of the cohort identified hallucinations five weeks into the term, underscoring both the prevalence of AI hallucinations and the need for stronger educational interventions to support students in discerning misinformation. 1 While rote memorisation and knowledge application play a role in learning, they appear less effective in recognising the subtle inaccuracies masked by AI's coherent and eloquent writing. Instead, strong synthesis and evaluation skills emerge as key indicators of hallucination detection. Gender differences are also notable, with female students more likely to detect AI errors, potentially due to their stronger academic performance in the course and a generally more cautious stance towards AI (Stein et al., 2024). We also show that students who identified hallucinations also expressed greater scepticism (more negative) towards AI. \n\nThe detection ability, nevertheless, did not translate into a greater likelihood of choosing an optional exam question on the same topic or achieving higher overall performance. Our specific, timely and coursework-related feedback strategy seems to have bridged the gap between students who initially succeed in detecting hallucinations and those who do not. This finding aligns with Transfer of Learning Theory (Perkins & Salomon, 1989), which suggests that structured reinforcement of learning experiences enables students to apply necessary skills in future settings of similar or more advanced nature. Feedback on AI hallucination detection serves as a scaffold for knowledge transfer, allowing students to refine their cognitive strategies and approach similar tasks with increased confidence. \n\nWe make three key theoretical contributions to AI literacy, epistemic cognition, and management education theory. First, we extend epistemic cognition theory (Chinn et al., 2011) by empirically examining how students engage in knowledge evaluation and justification in AI-integrated learning environments. Prior research has primarily focused on epistemic cognition in traditional educational settings, but ours applies to the novel challenge of AI hallucination.",
