      "snippet": {
        "text": "The method employed in this research is a quantitative type with a quasi-experimental design [57] and the matching-only pretest-posttest control design approach [58]. This study involved selecting two sample classes from the available population. Both classes have 30 students were given a pre-test before the treatment was administered. The first class, designated as the experimental group, received treatment in the form of the integrated PjBL-STEAM-SSI learning model, while the second class, designated as the control group, received a different treatment involving the application of conventional methods. After the treatment was administered, both classes underwent a post-test to measure the effectiveness of the applied learning model. An overview of the research method used is presented in Table 1 below: The implementation of the integrated PjBL-STEAM-SSI learning model in the experimental class was conducted for two cycles over four sessions. Each cycle of the integrated PjBL-STEAM-SSI learning model consisted of five phases, namely Reflection, Research, Discovery, Application, and Communication [60]. The first cycle of this learning process took place over two sessions, focusing on the topic of energy, while the second cycle focused on the topic of simple machines. The activities for each phase in each cycle were as follows. In the first phase, students were asked to reflect on the material, identify problems, conduct investigations, and describe the scientific presentation of photo/video problems, supporting the achievement of scientific literacy indicators such as \"explaining phenomena scientifically\". Then, in the second phase, students were required to conduct literature studies to gather as much relevant information as possible and design creative solutions to presented problems, facilitating the attainment of scientific literacy benchmarks such as \"assessing and formulating scientific inquiries\". Moreover, students also conducted concept validation through a simple laboratory experiment, supporting the achievement of scientific literacy indicators such as \"interpreting data and evidence scientifically\".",
