        "text": "Furthermore, conventional knowledge-building practices in physics such as argumentation and modeling are iterative in nature, i.e., these practices involve iterative refinement of arguments in making sense of novel contexts [5,54]. \n\nThis study makes several contributions to the contemporary literature focused on the intersection of artificial intelligence in STEM education. Firstly, this study goes beyond gauging the accuracy of AI responses to analyzing them through the lens of cognitive processes that are central to scientific inquiry. We adopt a frameworkbased approach in unpacking the subtle features of scientific inquiry in AI generated solutions and comparing them with students' responses. Secondly, though several studies have explored ChatGPT's causal reasoning in diverse domains such as neuropathic pain diagnosis [55] and publicly available multilingual multimodal data [56], this study explores mechanistic reasoning (which subsumes causal reasoning) in physics with a focus on diagrams and equations. \n\nOur findings however accompany several limitations. Firstly, the claims made in this study are based on the analysis of limited number of student and AI responses to just one physics problem. Analysis of a larger data set of responses to a diverse physics problems would undoubtedly shed more insights. Secondly, the students interview involved interventions from the interviewer during prolonged pauses and may have potentially impacted the participants' \"chain of thought\". We acknowledge this inherent limitation of think-aloud interview and in our approach to data collection. Finally, the data collected from ChatGPT and that from students are categorically of different nature. While students' data consisted of verbal arguments, gestures, and written solutions, ChatGPT's data consisted entirely of texts. Comparing textual data across the two would have been an ideal scenario. However, our primary objective was to explore how students naturally reason about a context and how AI would respond to the same context. As practitioners, we were aware that students would not prefer to write every argument in their solutions, particularly to as complex problem as the one described in this paper. Thus, we acknowledge that a major limitation of this work corresponds to making claims by analyzing two categorically different forms of data. \n\nFuture work would involve analyzing AI and student solutions to other problems from our data through the lens of additional processes such as modeling and argumentation that are integral to scientific reasoning.",
