      "snippet": {
        "text": "In this section, we present results from an evaluation study of two \"proto-iVFTs,\" with curricular support, that were created between 2013 and 2015 for use in middle and high school classrooms (Taylor et al., 2016). These proto-iVFTs lacked the adaptive feedback of a true iVFT, but classroom evaluation of these early designs was instructive for the later development (the remainder of this section will use VFT to refer to the proto-iVFTs). Building and testing these VFTs helped us to identify ways to track and respond to student interactions and ultimately to provide a stronger and more robust learning experience in the later iVFTs, such as the one presented in the main section of this article. \n\nTeachers were introduced to the VFTs through inperson summer professional development. Teachers then used the VFTs in their classrooms the following year and attended another professional development workshop the following summer to give more feedback on the tool. Feedback on the lessons was collected through teacher surveys. In addition, student learning data and feedback were collected through inclass pre-and postlesson surveys. Surveys included questions to assess changes in students' content knowledge, including both factual and procedural (scientific inquiry skill) knowledge, as well as their basic understanding of the nature of science. On the postlesson survey, students were also asked about how engaging the VFTs were and how they contributed to their learning. When applicable, paired t-tests were used to compare students' average pre-and posttest scores. Descriptive statistics were used to assess Likert-scale and open-ended response items. \n\nMost of the educators in this pilot worked with students in grades 5 through 8, and one worked in an informal education setting. Examples of how they used the VFTs can be seen in Table S1 in the supplemental materials. The implementation of the VFT labs were highly variable in the amount of time taken, being used from less than an hour to an entire unit.",
